Subject: ffmpeg: the software HEVC decoder on every shipped target, not just linux-x64

Patch 0061 built the software HEVC decoder and wired the three decision points
that consult it, but enabled it in exactly one ffmpeg target configuration.
Verified at this revision: Chrome/linux/x64 is the only one of the 44
configuration directories whose config_components.h carries
CONFIG_HEVC_DECODER 1 and CONFIG_HEVC_PARSER 1. linux-arm64 and windows-x64
still ship 0, so on those two the binary contradicts its own persona.

Measured on a real GPU-less Linux x64 server running stock Chrome, which is
what the fork has to answer as:

  canPlayType('video/mp4; codecs="hev1.1.6.L93.B0"')  ""
  MediaSource.isTypeSupported(hev1)                   false
  mediaCapabilities.decodingInfo(hev1 1080p30)        supported:false

H.264 answers 'probably', true and supported:true on the same host. Both
admitted 152.0.7977.83 reference captures record the opposite for HEVC --
corpus/blocks/gpu/m4-max-chrome-ddcd5fb54ca1.json and
corpus/blocks/gpu/windows-chrome-20260910T140813Z-d04eff68ce21.json both have
canPlayType 'probably', isTypeSupported true and decodingInfo supported,
smooth and powerEfficient all true. Three page-visible answers, each one
function call, each disagreeing with the claimed operating system on the host
about 90% of deployments actually run.

WHAT 0061 ALREADY DOES, AND WHAT IT LEAVES PER-TARGET. Its mechanism is
platform-independent and already compiled everywhere: supported_types.cc
returns true for HEVC Main and Main10 when IsDecoderBuiltInVideoCodec says a
built-in decoder exists, that function asks avcodec_find_decoder(AV_CODEC_ID_
HEVC), ffmpeg_common.cc widens the codec allow list to "h264,hevc" on the same
condition, and ffmpeg_video_decoder.cc accepts the codec and rejects profiles
above Main10. Every one of those is a runtime query, so each answers correctly
the moment a target's own configuration registers the decoder. Nothing in this
patch touches that code. What 0061 leaves per-target is the ffmpeg
configuration itself, the two registration tables, and the GN source list.

Nothing here touches allcodecs.c or parsers.c either, and it does not need to:
they are in the universal source list at ffmpeg_generated.gni:90 and :147 and
compile on all four targets already. What differs per target is only which
configuration directory's codec_list.c the include path at
third_party/ffmpeg/BUILD.gn:14 resolves to. So the registration 0061 added was
never dead code -- it compiled everywhere and registered HEVC on one target.

THE SWITCH SET, DERIVED BY DIFF RATHER THAN BY HAND. Five macros distinguish
0061's patched linux/x64 configuration from the pristine one, and nothing else
does: CONFIG_BSWAPDSP, CONFIG_HEVCPARSE and CONFIG_HEVC_SEI in config.h and
config.asm, CONFIG_HEVC_DECODER and CONFIG_HEVC_PARSER in config_components.h
and config_components.asm, each 0 -> 1. The same five, and only those five, are
flipped here for Chrome/linux/arm64 and Chrome/win/x64, and the FFMPEG_
CONFIGURATION comment gains the same two configure flags at the same anchor the
generator uses. The two registration tables were byte-identical across all four
targets before this patch and are byte-identical after it.

The arm64 configuration has no config.asm or config_components.asm, because
nothing assembles nasm there; only the two headers exist and only they are
patched. windows-x64 reads Chrome/win/x64, not Chrome/win-msvc/x64:
ffmpeg_options.gni:63 sets os_config = current_os and overrides it for Windows
only under is_win && !is_clang, and build/args/windows-x64.gn:5 sets
is_clang = true.

THE ARM64 ASM QUESTION. 0061 pulls in an x86 asm wrapper for bswapdsp. There is
no arm64 equivalent and none is added: bswapdsp.c:42-45 dispatches only
#if ARCH_RISCV / #elif ARCH_X86 && HAVE_X86ASM, so on aarch64 the C
implementation in that file is what runs and there is nothing to assemble.

What arm64 does need is its own HEVC SIMD, and it needs it unconditionally.
hevc/dsp.c:262 calls ff_hevc_dsp_init_aarch64 and hevc/pred.c:82 calls
ff_hevc_pred_init_aarch64 inside plain #if ARCH_AARCH64, with no HAVE_NEON
guard, so both symbols must link the moment HEVC is compiled for that
architecture. libavcodec/aarch64/Makefile:79-87 lists nine objects for
CONFIG_HEVC_DECODER, three of them under aarch64/h26x -- epel_neon.S,
qpel_neon.S and sao_neon.S -- which a search for "hevc" in that directory does
not find. Omitting them leaves 355 undefined ff_hevc_put_hevc_* and
ff_hevc_sao_edge_filter_* symbols; with them the only unresolved HEVC symbol is
ff_hevc_profiles, which libavcodec/profiles.c already supplies to every target
from ffmpeg_generated.gni:149.

THE GN CONDITION. 0061's stanza mixes the arch-independent HEVC core with
x86-only asm, so its predicate cannot be widened along the OS axis without also
widening it along the arch axis and handing libavcodec/x86/hevc/*.asm to arm64.
Instead this follows the shape the rest of the generated file already has -- one
block per disjoint source set with a disjunctive condition, as at lines 302,
345, 378, 413 and 468 -- keyed on os_config exactly as 0061 is: an
arch-independent block for the two new targets, an x86 block, an aarch64 block.
0061's stanza is left alone and stays disjoint from all three, so no target
receives a source name twice. os_config "linux-noasm" is excluded from every
one of them, which keeps the MSAN configuration free of these asm-dependent
objects for the same reason 0061 excluded it.

build_ffmpeg.py, the generator of record, is widened to the same three targets.
Leaving it at linux/x64 would mean a regeneration silently produced
configurations that contradict the ones in the tree.

MACOS IS DELIBERATELY NOT INCLUDED. It does not have the defect.
video_toolbox_video_decoder.cc:514-535 declares HEVCPROFILE_MIN through
HEVCPROFILE_MAX supported on any macOS host, gated only on the
kPlatformHEVCDecoderSupport feature -- enabled by default at
media_switches.cc:418 -- and the disable_accelerated_hevc_decode workaround. It
probes no hardware, and its own comment says why: "because we allow the OS
software decoder we report the minimum possible HEVC encode size". So
VideoToolbox answers for HEVC on every Apple host with or without a usable
media engine, the supplemental profile cache is populated, and the three
surfaces already answer as the captures do. Adding a second software decoder
there would be weight with no observable effect.

A HOST WITH HARDWARE HEVC KEEPS USING IT. This adds no decoder to the selection
list: DefaultDecoderFactory::CreateVideoDecoders already pushes
FFmpegVideoDecoder unconditionally at default_decoder_factory.cc:159-162, after
the external GPU-backed factory at :138-148, and selection takes the first
entry that initialises. Only its answer to IsCodecSupported(kHEVC) changes, and
that answer is consulted only if selection reaches it. In the three places
where a hardware decoder consults IsDecoderBuiltInVideoCodec -- d3d11_video_
decoder.cc:383, video_toolbox_video_decoder.cc:151 and mojo_video_decoder.cc:
164 -- the predicate is read as "is there a software fallback", and each is
evaluated only after the hardware path has already reported that it does not
support the config. Hardware that reports support never reaches the branch.
The software decoder is a floor, not a replacement.

At the ffmpeg level codec_list[] and parser_list[] gain exactly one HEVC entry
each on targets that had none, so avcodec_find_decoder(AV_CODEC_ID_HEVC) has a
single unambiguous match and nothing is registered twice.

NO NEW OBSERVABLE. No command-line switch, no feature flag, no console message,
no histogram. The 0108 trap -- re-enabling a fallback also re-enabling a
deprecation warning into every page's console -- has no counterpart here: the
media stack has no AddConsoleMessage at all, and the only logging the HEVC path
adds is MEDIA_LOG on decode failure, which is the same path H.264 has always
used and is not reached by a capability query. canPlayType, isTypeSupported and
decodingInfo call the supported-types tables and log nothing.

diff --git a/media/ffmpeg/scripts/build_ffmpeg.py b/media/ffmpeg/scripts/build_ffmpeg.py
--- a/media/ffmpeg/scripts/build_ffmpeg.py
+++ b/media/ffmpeg/scripts/build_ffmpeg.py
@@ -995,7 +995,14 @@
         '--enable-parser=aac,h264',
     ])
 
-    if target_os == 'linux' and target_arch == 'x64':
+    # Every target whose checked-in config carries the software HEVC decoder.
+    # Keep this in step with the per-target stanzas in ffmpeg_generated.gni: a
+    # regeneration that omitted a target here would silently produce a config
+    # that contradicts the one in the tree. 'linux-noasm' is deliberately absent
+    # because it builds without assembly, and 'mac' because VideoToolbox already
+    # answers for HEVC on every Apple host.
+    if (target_os, target_arch) in (('linux', 'x64'), ('linux', 'arm64'),
+                                    ('win', 'x64')):
         configure_flags['Chrome'].extend([
             '--enable-decoder=hevc', '--enable-parser=hevc',
         ])
